                <head>No. 8<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi></head>
                <p>Telegram 248 to Secretary Dulles in Dhahran, May 18, repeated to Cairo, reported
                    concern in the Department of State that military moves by both Egyptians and
                    British would result in an incident which could set off a chain reaction.
                    Telegram 2456 from Cairo, May 19, advised the Department that the likelihood of
                    an immediate “blow-up” had been considerably diminished as a result of the
                    Secretary’s visit. Telegram 6235 from London, May 22, reported, <pb facs="0069"
                        n="29" xml:id="pg29"/> however, that the British Foreign Office considered
                    its warning to British subjects to leave Egypt amply justified by events.
                    Documentation is in the Conference files, lot 59 D 95, CF 156. For further
                    information on this topic, see <ref target="frus:frus1952-54v09p2/d947"
                        >Documents 947</ref> ff.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
